**Proof.**

Let the beads in cyclic order have labels $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$ (indices taken modulo $n$), with $\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = n-1$.

**Setup.** Define $y_i = a_i - 1$ for each $i$. Then
$$\sum_{i=1}^n y_i = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i - n = (n-1) - n = -1.$$
The desired inequality $\sum_{i=1}^k x_i \le k-1$ for a linear ordering $x_1, \dots, x_n$ is equivalent (with $x_i = a_{\sigma(i)}$ for the chosen cut) to
$$\sum_{i=1}^k y_{\sigma(i)} \le -1 \qquad \text{for all } k = 1, \dots, n. \tag{$\star$}$$

**Choosing the cut.** Fix any bead as position $1$ and define partial sums
$$S_0 = 0, \qquad S_k = \sum_{i=1}^k y_i \quad (1 \le k \le n),$$
so $S_n = -1$. Since the $a_i$ (hence $y_i$) are integers, every $S_k$ is an integer.

Let $M = \max_{0 \le k \le n-1} S_k$, and let
$$p = \max\{\, k \in \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\} : S_k = M \,\}$$
be the **last** index at which this maximum is attained. Cut the necklace after position $p$, producing the linear string
$$a_{p+1},\; a_{p+2},\; \dots,\; a_n,\; a_1,\; \dots,\; a_p.$$

**Verification of ($\star$).** For this ordering, the prefix sum of the first $k$ terms (in the $y$-variables) is
$$P_k = \sum_{i=1}^k y_{p+i},$$
where indices are read cyclically. We must show $P_k \le -1$ for every $k \in \{1, \dots, n\}$, i.e., $S_{p+k} - S_p \le -1$, i.e. (using integrality) $S_{p+k} < S_p = M$.

We distinguish two cases.

*Case 1: $p + k \le n$.* Then $p + k \in \{p+1, \dots, n\}$.

- If $p+k \le n-1$: since $p$ is the **last** index achieving the maximum $M$ and $p+k > p$, we have $S_{p+k} < M = S_p$. ✓
- If $p+k = n$: $S_n = -1$. Since $M \ge S_0 = 0 > -1$, we get $S_n = -1 < M = S_p$. ✓

*Case 2: $p + k > n$.* Write $p + k = n + r$ with $r = p + k - n$. Since $n - p < k \le n$, we have $1 \le r \le p$. Using the cyclic structure,
$$S_{p+k} = S_{n+r} = S_n + S_r = -1 + S_r.$$
We need $-1 + S_r < S_p$, i.e. (by integrality) $S_r \le S_p = M$. Since $r \in \{1, \dots, p\} \subseteq \{0, \dots, n-1\}$ and $M$ is the maximum, $S_r \le M = S_p$. ✓

In both cases $P_k \le -1$, so ($\star$) holds. Translating back via $y_i = a_i - 1$:
$$\sum_{i=1}^k x_i = \sum_{i=1}^k a_{p+i} = k + P_k \le k + (-1) = k - 1$$
for every $k = 1, 2, \dots, n$.

Thus the cut after position $p$ yields a linear string satisfying all the required prefix-sum inequalities. $\blacksquare$

